 \section{Discussion and Conclusions}
 \label{sec:discussion}
+\subsection{How representation choices affect measurement}
+\label{sec:representation_findings}
+The specific contribution beyond existing video-to-log construction is the account of a shared factory representation and its analytical consequences. Vid2log already connects visual interpretation, domain context, and event data~\cite{gavric2024multimodal}. Event-abstraction and quality-aware construction research already establish that representation and input quality matter~\cite{vanZelst2021abstraction,andrews2020quality}. Our existing artifacts show how three decisions interact: what the vocabulary names, how episodes are assigned to those names, and how the labels are mapped into measurement categories.
+
+Factory 003 separates these decisions directly. Its activity vocabulary names transport, S4 assigns 101 events to two transport activities, and the process-based map assigns their durations to categories other than TR. The zero TR share is therefore explained by aggregation, not a missing transport term. A vocabulary check alone would not detect this mismatch between the analytical question and the measurement rule.
+
+The foundry example shows a different dependency. A 31-second description becomes packing and leveling events lasting 19 and 12 seconds. The factory lists supply reusable names, but the assignment step also supplies an internal split not timed separately in the narration. An analysis of the two actions' duration shares depends on this additional boundary decision. The transcript audit supports the leveling description without independently validating the split.
+
+Finally, S5 retains video-relative offsets while placing clips in filename order on a synthetic worker timeline. A worker case can therefore add a transition across a clip join, whereas a video case preserves that boundary. This follows from the merge rule and shows why case selection is part of the measurement definition. These examples demonstrate particular representational effects; they do not estimate how often each effect occurs in other datasets or establish a more accurate alternative pipeline.
+
 \subsection{Answers to the research questions}
 For RQ1, the five-stage pipeline produced a layered resource of transcripts, summaries, vocabularies, and 322 event logs. All 10{,}698 event rows satisfy the reported field, ordering, and label checks. Overlaps and unchecked semantic properties show why these checks must be named rather than treated as general validity.
 
