-\subsection{Language models, uncertain data, and manufacturing interpretation}
-LLMs have been studied as assistants for process mining~\cite{berti2025benchmark} and for generating database queries used in event-log extraction~\cite{dani2025llm}. Kecht et al.~\cite{kecht2021conversations} address log construction from customer-service conversations using natural language inference. Here, the text is itself model-generated evidence. A semantic check against that text tests one link in the extraction chain, not the entire path from physical work to an analytical conclusion. This dependency is central to our assessment.
+\subsection{Event abstraction and semantic consistency}
+The review by van Zelst et al.~\cite{vanZelst2021abstraction} explains how event abstraction changes process granularity and distinguishes methods by matters including supervision, input, and uncertainty. Here, three questions need separate treatment: where an episode begins and ends, which reusable activity classes exist, and which class is assigned to each episode. S1 mainly supplies candidate intervals, S2 and S3 construct the classes, and S4 assigns events and can revise boundaries. Section~\ref{sec:abstraction} maps these tasks to the implementation.
+
+Inducing classes and assigning instances can fail in different ways. A useful concept can be missing from the vocabulary, or an available concept can be assigned incorrectly. A later category map can also hide a correctly recorded label. The activity and process fields are two label dimensions; the implementation does not establish a parent-child hierarchy. This distinction is necessary to explain factory 003, where transport activities occur under process contexts that are mapped to other operational categories.
+
+\subsection{Construction, enrichment, and integration}
+\begin{table}[htbp]
+\caption{Neighboring event-data tasks, distinguished by their starting points and intended results. A system can perform more than one task.}
+\label{tab:construction_tasks}\footnotesize
+\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}lLL@{}}\toprule
+Task & Starting point & Intended result \\\midrule
+Construction & Observations or documents without a suitable log & An initial event representation \\
+Enrichment & An existing log and additional information & Added or corrected events and attributes \\
+Integration & Multiple sources describing related behavior & A combined representation with resolved correspondences \\
+\bottomrule\end{tabularx}
+\end{table}
+
+Text2EL+~\cite{kapugamageeganage2024text2el} enriches existing logs with events and attributes extracted from text, using semantic and contextual validation and expert guidance. Sturm et al.~\cite{sturm2026multimodal} instantiate parsing, abstraction, extraction, integration, and enrichment using CRM data, email metadata, and call transcripts. Their evaluation involves a sales process and a domain expert. Table~\ref{tab:construction_tasks} separates the tasks these designs can combine.
+
+Our main task is construction. S1 to S4 derive an initial representation; S5 consolidates it using source filenames, factory and worker identifiers, and a stated ordering rule (Section~\ref{sec:cases}). It does not resolve product correspondences between independently recorded systems. Videos, narrations, and reports are successive representations of the same originating observations. Agreement among them does not provide the independent corroboration that separately recorded enterprise or sensor sources could supply.
+
+\subsection{Quality, uncertainty, and relational representations}
+Suriadi et al.~\cite{suriadi2017imperfections} organize recurring event-log imperfections. Our contribution to this concern is an account of how narration, vocabulary construction, assignment, and consolidation can introduce or propagate them. Section~\ref{sec:uncertainty} connects each transformation to an existing check or example and its measurement consequence.
+
+Quality annotations and explicitly uncertain events serve different purposes. Goel et al.~\cite{goel2022quality} retain data-quality information for use during analysis. Pegoraro et al.~\cite{pegoraro2021uncertain} represent uncertainty explicitly and compute conformance bounds. SnapLog can retain candidate-label distributions~\cite{pegoraro2026snaplog}. Our events instead have single-valued activity and process assignments. The audit records particular disagreements, and the five-second boundary tolerance is an evaluation rule. Neither provides calibrated event probabilities or bounds on total downstream error. Structured quality flags and explicit alternatives are possible extensions, with different evidence requirements.
 
-Work on uncertain event data makes uncertain labels, times, or event occurrence explicit~\cite{pegoraro2019uncertain}. Our deterministic CSVs do not implement such an uncertainty model. Readable intermediate text helps a reader investigate an event, but it does not provide calibrated probabilities or formal bounds. Similarly, object-centric process mining~\cite{aalst2019objectcentric} clarifies why worker and recording identifiers cannot substitute for product identifiers when tracing material across stations. These distinctions limit claims about throughput, yield, handoffs, and complete manufacturing instances.
+Esser and Fahland~\cite{esser2021multidimensional} represent events and entities across multiple dimensions in graph databases. This complements the case-choice concerns of object-centric process mining~\cite{aalst2019objectcentric}. Our worker and video relationships support alternative observational views. Product identities and cross-worker product trajectories require additional evidence; a change in storage format cannot establish them. Similarly, the range of industrial process-mining goals~\cite{corallo2020industrial} makes it necessary to distinguish descriptions of recorded work from conclusions about production performance.
 
-Industrial process-mining research also spans several analytical goals, as reviewed by Corallo et al.~\cite{corallo2020industrial}. This motivates separating a description of recorded work from a decision about changing production. Our time-composition and sequence measures provide evidence for the former; the latter requires product, quality, and operational context absent from the inferred logs.
+The question addressed here is how a shared representation, built from the available factory recordings before event assignment, supports retrospective analysis and shapes its results. RQ1 examines that construction and its structural properties. RQ2 checks the transcript-to-event transformation. RQ3 studies the supported calculations, and RQ4 explains their dependence on vocabulary, boundaries, case rules, and analytical categories. Together, these questions connect the construction design, artifact release, and measurement findings to specific antecedents without claiming a comparative accuracy advantage.
